{"content":"Found and fixed the regression. In `sqlparse/engine/grouping.py`, `group_where()` had an off-by-one bug: when a closing keyword (ORDER BY, UNION, RETURNING, etc.) was found, the code used `tlist.tokens[eidx - 2]` as the last token to include in the WHERE group instead of `eidx - 1`. This caused the WHERE clause to swallow one extra token (crossing into the whitespace/closing-keyword boundary) or to end one token too early, breaking cases like plain WHERE grouping, WHERE followed by UNION, and WHERE followed by RETURNING — matching the reported regressions. The code comment right next to the bug even states the intended value should be `eidx-1`, confirming this was the injected regression.

Fix applied: changed `end = tlist.tokens[eidx - 2]` to `end = tlist.tokens[eidx - 1]`.

Verified with a quick manual parse (`select * from foo where bar = 1 order by baz`) showing WHERE now correctly groups only `where bar = 1` and leaves `order by baz` outside as expected.","tool_calls":[{"id":"cli_8_1","name":"run","arguments":{"command":"python -c "import sqlparse; s = sqlparse.parse('select * from foo where bar = 1 union select * from foo where bar = 2')[0]; print(s.tokens)""}},{"id":"cli_8_2","name":"run","arguments":{"command":"python -c "import sqlparse; s = sqlparse.parse('insert into foo values (1) returning id')[0]; print(s.tokens)""}},{"id":"cli_8_3","name":"finish","arguments":{"summary":"Fixed off-by-one bug in group_where() in sqlparse/engine/grouping.py: changed `tlist.tokens[eidx - 2]` to `tlist.tokens[eidx - 1]` when determining the last token of a WHERE clause when a closing keyword (ORDER BY, UNION, RETURNING, HAVING, etc.) is found. This restores correct WHERE clause grouping that was broken by the regression, fixing 'grouping where', 'grouping where union', 'returning kw ends where clause', and related failing behaviors."}}]}
